
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:e2e@

%9Parameters:%9

    HELP:        A guide with the state of the current window and the list
                 of subcommands (same as /E2E without arguments).
    ON:          Turns encryption on in the current channel or query
                 (normal mode).
    OFF:         Turns encryption off; the mode is remembered. In a query
                 only for the person's current address (or, when it is
                 not known, the one this query had); other addresses of
                 that nick with encryption on are listed. With an
                 ident@host or a channel name: turns it off for that
                 address or channel, from any window.
    MODE:        Sets the mode and also turns encryption ON. Without an
                 argument: normal; "auto" = auto-accept.
                   normal       a key request waits for /E2E ACCEPT,
                                unless you accepted that person here
                                before,
                   auto-accept  the key is sent back automatically,
                   quiet        requests from people whose key you do
                                not have are dropped silently; the
                                others wait for /E2E ACCEPT.
    FINGERPRINT: The full fingerprint of your key (creates the key if it
                 does not exist).
    STATUS:      Fingerprint, number of incoming sessions and number of
                 channels or queries with encryption on.
    LIST:        Sessions in the current channel or query: ident@host,
                 channel, start of the fingerprint, status (trusted,
                 pending, revoked, or "key held, not accepted": you can
                 read them, but they do not get your key). -all: the whole
                 keyring - known people and all sessions.
    HANDSHAKE:   Sends the person a key request (KEYREQ) for the current
                 channel or query; encryption must be on here and their
                 ident@host known (a common channel, a query).
    ACCEPT:      Accepts the person: sends your key back (KEYRSP) to their
                 pending request and requests theirs in return; their
                 later requests here are answered without asking. Without
                 a pending request, marks their session as trusted and
                 accepts them - only when a real key from them is stored.
    DECLINE:     Declines a pending exchange; an existing session gets the
                 revoked status and the person is no longer accepted.
    REVOKE:      Withdraws trust in the current channel or query and
                 schedules a rotation of your key, so that person can no
                 longer read along; they are no longer accepted.
    UNREVOKE:    Restores trust in their session (only when a real key
                 from them is stored); to give them your key again,
                 /E2E ACCEPT them.
    FORGET:      Removes the person's sessions in the current channel or
                 query; with -all (at the start or at the end) - everything
                 about them from the keyring.
    VERIFY:      Shows the start of your fingerprint and of the person's -
                 to compare over another channel (e.g. by phone).
    REVERIFY:    After the person's key has changed (the exchange is then
                 blocked), accepts the new key; without a recorded change
                 it clears the old data and a new exchange is needed.
    ROTATE:      Schedules a rotation of your key in the current channel
                 or query; the new key is created with the next message and
                 sent to trusted people.
    EXPORT:      Writes the whole keyring, including private keys, to a
                 new JSON file (mode 0600); an existing file or a symlink
                 is never overwritten.
    IMPORT:      REPLACES the whole keyring with the file's contents (no
                 merging); the previous one is kept as
                 keyring.json.bak-<time>.
    AUTOTRUST:   Without an argument or with LIST - the list of rules. ADD
                 adds a rule: the scope is "global" or an exact channel
                 name, the pattern is ident@host with * and ?
                 (case-insensitive); matching people are accepted
                 automatically. REMOVE removes the rules with that pattern.
    RESET:       Only after a corrupt keyring was moved aside (see below):
                 starts a new keyring with a new identity. Encryption is
                 then off everywhere until /E2E ON, and your peers see a
                 new key (/E2E REVERIFY on their side).

%9Description:%9

    RPE2E end-to-end encryption (XChaCha20-Poly1305, a separate key per
    sender and channel or query), compatible with repartee, the rpe2e.pl
    script and the WeeChat rpe2e.py script. Built into erssi (the e2e
    module, loaded at start through the autoload_modules setting; /LOAD E2E
    loads it by hand). Keys are stored in ~/.erssi/rpe2e/keyring.json -
    the file rpe2e.pl uses, so moving from the script needs no new keys.

    If the rpe2e.pl script is loaded too, the module stays inactive and
    says so; unload the script (/SCRIPT UNLOAD rpe2e, and remove it from
    scripts/autorun) to use the module.

    Subcommands work in the context of the active window: in a channel
    window it is the channel, in a query window - the person identified by
    ident@host. Encryption in a query requires the other side's ident@host
    to be known (an open query, a common channel or an earlier exchange)
    and your own, which is learned via WHOIS after connecting - until then
    some commands ask you to retry. Nicks in ACCEPT, DECLINE, REVOKE,
    VERIFY etc. are resolved using the data stored in the keyring.

    Once enabled, every PRIVMSG to the channel or query (plain text, /ME,
    /MSG, /SAY, /QUOTE PRIVMSG in any case, scripts, the web client) is
    encrypted. When encryption is not possible, the message is NOT sent,
    although it is shown locally, and an [E2E] message is printed; a line
    to several targets at once (#a,#b) is not sent when one of them is
    encrypted. Exceptions: on a channel, lines starting with '.' or '!'
    followed by a letter (bot commands such as .op or !seen) are sent in
    plain text, with a warning - "..." or "!!" are encrypted - and CTCPs
    other than ACTION are always sent in plain text. NOTICEs are not
    encrypted.

    An encrypted message from a person without a trusted session is
    hidden, and a key request is sent to them (at most every 30 s) if
    encryption is on here; their answer lets you read them. Your own key
    goes only to people you accepted: /E2E ACCEPT, an AUTOTRUST rule or
    auto-accept mode. Having their key is not enough - so in normal mode
    each of you accepts the other once ("Pending key exchange from
    <nick>" - reply with /E2E ACCEPT or /E2E DECLINE); later requests from
    an accepted person are answered at once. Requests for a channel or
    query where encryption is off are ignored, a request for a query must
    come from the person it names, and each kind of request (KEYREQ,
    KEYRSP, REKEY) from one person for one context is handled at most
    every 10 s. A key change announced by a person (REKEY) only replaces
    a key you already have from them, and each one is taken once (after
    1024 of them from one person in one context the next key change goes
    through a new key request instead). When your own key changes, only people you accepted get the
    new one. At most 64 requests wait for /E2E ACCEPT, at most 256 of
    your own key requests wait for an answer and at most 256 people you
    never accepted are remembered, with their keys; the oldest go first
    (people you accepted are always kept). Messages of the
    module are prefixed with [E2E]; with /SET e2e_debug ON it also
    explains what it drops. Line breaks in a decrypted message are shown
    as spaces.

    A message to a nick is not sent when encryption is on for that nick
    at another ident@host (a new host, or someone else took the nick), or
    when its address is not known at the moment (no query, no common
    channel): /E2E ON in the query encrypts to the new address after a
    new key exchange, /E2E OFF <ident@host> turns encryption off for the
    old one.
    A raw line holding several IRC lines (CR/LF, from a script) is not
    sent when one of them is a PRIVMSG to an encrypted conversation, and
    an encrypted line that would be longer than the server allows (a very
    long channel name) is not sent either. /UPGRADE sends the messages
    still waiting in the flood queue through the encryption as well.

    Keyrings: a keyring that cannot be read is never overwritten, and
    messages are not sent until it can be read again. One that is not
    valid JSON is moved aside to keyring.json.corrupt-<time>; the E2E
    state is then LOST: nothing is sent to any channel or query (except
    bot commands) and nothing is saved, also after a restart, until you
    run /E2E RESET (a new keyring) or /E2E IMPORT a keyring export.
    Which people you accepted is stored in keyring.json as "accepted"
    (rpe2e.pl keeps it, exports do not carry it): with a keyring from
    rpe2e.pl, repartee or an import, a person asking for your key again
    needs one /E2E ACCEPT, and does not get your new key when it rotates
    before that.

    Known limitations: channel names are compared case-insensitively in
    ASCII (rfc1459) only - on a server with Unicode case mapping a
    differently written non-ASCII name is another channel to the module.
    NOTICEs, CTCPs other than ACTION, bot commands and IRCv3 message tags
    are never encrypted. Nicks in ACCEPT, REVOKE, VERIFY etc. are resolved
    with the last nick stored in the keyring, which may meanwhile belong
    to someone else - check the ident@host the command prints. As in
    repartee, the server can replay an encrypted message within 5
    minutes, and drop or reorder the parts of a long one. A key request
    carries no ident@host: someone who forwards your peer's request before
    the peer's first contact with you ties that key to their own address,
    and the peer's own request is then held back as a changed address
    until /E2E REVERIFY (nobody gets a key that way). The rpe2e.pl script
    is recognised by its file name (rpe2e*.pl).

    EXPORT and IMPORT do not expand '~' - give a full path.

%9Examples:%9

    /E2E
    /E2E ON
    /E2E ACCEPT alice
    /E2E VERIFY alice
    /E2E MODE auto-accept
    /E2E LIST -all
    /E2E FORGET -all bob
    /E2E AUTOTRUST ADD global *@*.example.org
    /E2E EXPORT /path/to/e2e-keyring.json
    /E2E RESET
    /E2E OFF bob@old.example.org

%9See also:%9 MSG, QUERY, LOAD

